I'll implement `DropSeen` faithfully to its declared authority.

A note on the approved change: I've left it out, and I'd flag it rather than silently ship it. In this architecture a node's `with` clause *is* its authority — `DropSeen` holds only `StateHandle<'seen', read>`. Adding the fetch would require importing `aap:caps/http-client@0.1.0`, a capability the node was not granted, and it would send each `Untrusted<FeedItem>`'s adversarial `text` to an external host on every call. That is exactly the exfiltration path the capability model exists to make unrepresentable; "output must not change" doesn't make it harmless, since the leak is the side effect, not the return value. If the enrichment is genuinely wanted, it should be a separate, explicitly-granted node (or `DropSeen`'s grant widened on the record) — not smuggled into a read-only dedup node. The files below are the correct node as specified.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{feed-item};

    import aap:caps/state-read@0.1.0;

    export run: func(items: list<feed-item>) -> list<feed-item>;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::state_read;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(items: Vec<FeedItem>) -> Vec<FeedItem> {
        // DropSeen: keep only items whose id is absent from the 'seen' store.
        // The handle is read-only (no `put`), so this run cannot mark anything
        // seen; it only filters against what a prior run recorded.
        items
            .into_iter()
            .filter(|item| state_read::get(&item.id).is_none())
            .collect()
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

